// @emit-error `is` test but no union type declared
// @hint redundant type annotation: `a` is inferred as `{v: i32}[]`
// FALSE-REJECT xfail. This program is SOUND and SHOULD compile; it errors. NOT an
// unsoundness — the compiler refuses a valid program. The `xfail-false-reject-`
// prefix is the corpus's rejects-too-much kind; see README.vl. The `@error` pins the
// UNDESIRED diagnostic so the gap's closure is loud rather than silent.
//
// THE GAP: an INLINE union whose arms are arrays of STRUCTURALLY IDENTICAL structs.
// A union's members are deduped structurally, so `A[] | B[]` — where `A` and `B` are
// both `{v:i32}` — collapses to a single member and takes that member's type. The
// annotation node then records a plain `TyArray`, not a `TyUnion`.
//
// `emit_collect`'s array rung skips array VALIDATION for a union whose last arm is an
// array (its ref-array arms are interned by `markRefArrayArms` on the union path).
// That skip asks "is this node's type a union?" — and for the collapsed shape the
// honest answer is no, so the union spelling `A[]|B[]` falls into `checkArrName` and
// is rejected as a non-i32 array.
//
// Three neighbours all compile clean, which is what makes this the narrow case:
//   - DIFFERENT shapes, inline:  `A[] | C[]`   (members survive dedup -> a real union)
//   - SAME shapes, DECLARED:     `type Both = A[] | B[]`  (matched by name/index)
//   - SAME shapes, non-array:    `Cat | Dog`   (B107 — declines to the name, correctly)
//
// PRE-EXISTING: reproduced on the compiler at 08647012^, i.e. before the arena union
// rung (#1592) existed, so it is not a regression from that conversion. It is the
// same root cause B107 measured at the variant param rung — structural dedup erasing
// a distinction the spelling still carries — surfacing here as a reject instead of a
// wrong answer.
//
// EXPECTED: compiles clean and prints 1. ACTUAL: the emit error pinned above.
//
// TWO FIXES HAVE BEEN TRIED AND MEASURED. Neither works; both are recorded so a third
// attempt starts further along.
//
// 1. TEACH THE ARRAY RUNG THE TREE (B133, SHIPPED in B165). `emit_collect`'s array rung
//    claimed this annotation only through `nameIsArray`'s trailing-`]` false positive;
//    reading `tsKind[root] == TS_ARR` correctly declines it. That did not fix the reject, it
//    MOVED it — which is why the `@emit-error` above now pins "`is` test but no union type
//    declared" rather than the array message. The relocation is an improvement even though
//    the gap remains: the error now names the real cause (the collapsed union was never
//    registered) instead of blaming the rung where it first surfaced.
//
// 2. REGISTER THE COLLAPSED ARRAY UNION (B135). `registerCollapsedUnionName` exists for
//    exactly this destruction, but its guard is `if t is TyObj` — a same-shape ARRAY union
//    collapses to a plain `TyArray` (the dedup runs on the ELEMENTS, so the arms become
//    identical arrays and the union degenerates one level down), so the guard misses it.
//    Extending the guard to `TyArray` removes this reject and produces INVALID WASM:
//
//        vl check --codegen  =>  rc 0
//        vl run              =>  Invalid input WebAssembly code at offset 197:
//                                type mismatch: expected (ref null $type), found (ref $type)
//
//    Strictly worse: a loud reject becomes a check-clean invalid module, the same signature
//    B109 measured when trusting the arena at the variant param rung. Registering the
//    SPELLING restores the classification but not the BOX — the union's ref-array arms are
//    interned by `markRefArrayArms` on the union path, which the collapsed type never takes.
//
// So the real fix is upstream of both: the collapse must preserve the emitter's boxing
// shape, not merely be re-labelled afterwards. That is B109's prescription (record the
// lowering decision) applied to the array case.
type A = { v: i32 }
type B = { v: i32 }

function pick(xs: A[] | B[]) {
  if xs is A[] { return 1 }
  return 2
}

const a: A[] = [{ v: 1 }]
print(pick(a))
